1
00:00:00,361 --> 00:00:03,071
Here are three sealed jars of colourless gas.

2
00:00:03,874 --> 00:00:05,078
One holds pure oxygen,

3
00:00:05,661 --> 00:00:08,471
one holds pure carbon dioxide, and one holds

4
00:00:08,632 --> 00:00:09,334
ordinary air.

5
00:00:10,077 --> 00:00:12,563
They look exactly the same, so looking won't

6
00:00:12,604 --> 00:00:13,446
tell you which is which.

7
00:00:14,329 --> 00:00:16,934
But chemists sort every sample into a few

8
00:00:17,035 --> 00:00:19,940
kinds, and the sorting doesn't depend on how

9
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:20,381
it looks.

10
00:00:21,105 --> 00:00:23,175
It depends on the particles inside.

11
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:24,016
So

12
00:00:24,537 --> 00:00:26,978
let's zoom in, and see how particles tell

13
00:00:26,999 --> 00:00:30,000
an element, a compound and a mixture apart.

14
00:00:30,802 --> 00:00:31,988
Start with the first split.

15
00:00:32,631 --> 00:00:35,178
A substance is one particular kind of matter,

16
00:00:35,358 --> 00:00:36,802
with its own kind of particle.

17
00:00:37,543 --> 00:00:40,870
In jar A, every particle is identical: two

18
00:00:41,012 --> 00:00:42,474
oxygen atoms joined together.

19
00:00:43,059 --> 00:00:44,391
Jar B is the same idea.

20
00:00:44,937 --> 00:00:47,584
Every particle is one carbon atom joined to

21
00:00:47,645 --> 00:00:48,767
two oxygen atoms.

22
00:00:49,591 --> 00:00:51,917
A sample that contains only one substance is

23
00:00:51,998 --> 00:00:53,361
called a pure substance.

24
00:00:54,205 --> 00:00:54,950
Now look at the air.

25
00:00:55,474 --> 00:00:57,877
There are different kinds of particle here: mostly

26
00:00:57,958 --> 00:01:01,063
nitrogen, some oxygen, a little argon and carbon

27
00:01:01,084 --> 00:01:01,584
dioxide,

28
00:01:02,105 --> 00:01:03,367
all moving around separately.

29
00:01:04,068 --> 00:01:06,194
A sample with two or more substances mixed

30
00:01:06,215 --> 00:01:07,718
together is a mixture.

31
00:01:08,382 --> 00:01:10,116
Pure substances split again.

32
00:01:10,903 --> 00:01:12,175
Oxygen is an element.

33
00:01:13,004 --> 00:01:14,834
An element is a substance made of only

34
00:01:15,016 --> 00:01:15,961
one kind of atom.

35
00:01:16,646 --> 00:01:18,810
Here, each particle happens to be a molecule,

36
00:01:19,252 --> 00:01:21,997
two atoms joined together, but both atoms are

37
00:01:22,118 --> 00:01:22,458
oxygen.

38
00:01:23,081 --> 00:01:25,391
So a molecule doesn't make something a compound.

39
00:01:26,321 --> 00:01:27,372
Iron is an element too.

40
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:29,941
In a piece of iron, every atom is

41
00:01:29,981 --> 00:01:30,704
an iron atom.

42
00:01:31,507 --> 00:01:33,932
Elements are the substances listed in the periodic

43
00:01:33,993 --> 00:01:34,373
table,

44
00:01:34,875 --> 00:01:37,199
and an element's formula has just one symbol.

45
00:01:37,921 --> 00:01:39,991
Carbon dioxide is a compound.

46
00:01:40,452 --> 00:01:42,456
A compound is a substance made of two

47
00:01:42,636 --> 00:01:45,101
or more elements, chemically bonded together in a

48
00:01:45,221 --> 00:01:45,963
fixed ratio.

49
00:01:46,844 --> 00:01:49,928
Every carbon dioxide particle has one carbon atom

50
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:53,034
bonded to two oxygen atoms, so the ratio

51
00:01:53,075 --> 00:01:54,536
is always one to two.

52
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:58,031
Water is a compound too, always two hydrogen

53
00:01:58,072 --> 00:01:59,357
atoms to one oxygen.

54
00:02:00,242 --> 00:02:02,607
Some compounds, like table salt, aren't made of

55
00:02:02,708 --> 00:02:03,971
separate molecules at all.

56
00:02:04,553 --> 00:02:07,799
Charged particles called ions, sodium and chloride, are

57
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:09,823
packed in a lattice, always one to one.

58
00:02:11,086 --> 00:02:13,654
That fixed ratio is what marks a compound.

59
00:02:14,258 --> 00:02:16,104
And because its atoms are bonded, you can

60
00:02:16,225 --> 00:02:18,453
only split a compound into its elements with

61
00:02:18,534 --> 00:02:19,557
a chemical reaction.

62
00:02:20,482 --> 00:02:21,568
A mixture is different.

63
00:02:22,071 --> 00:02:24,622
Its substances are just mingled, so each one

64
00:02:24,662 --> 00:02:26,851
keeps its own particles and its own properties.

65
00:02:27,915 --> 00:02:30,963
Here are iron filings stirred into yellow sulfur

66
00:02:30,984 --> 00:02:31,244
powder.

67
00:02:31,847 --> 00:02:33,932
The iron and the sulfur aren't bonded to

68
00:02:33,973 --> 00:02:35,858
each other, so a magnet can pull the

69
00:02:35,938 --> 00:02:36,619
iron straight out.

70
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:38,921
And there's no fixed recipe.

71
00:02:39,505 --> 00:02:41,090
You could mix in a pinch of sulfur

72
00:02:41,532 --> 00:02:43,478
or a whole spoonful, and it's still a

73
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:44,924
mixture of iron and sulfur.

74
00:02:45,747 --> 00:02:49,275
Proportions that can change are called variable composition,

75
00:02:49,717 --> 00:02:51,039
and that's a mark of a mixture.

76
00:02:51,809 --> 00:02:52,493
Quick prediction.

77
00:02:53,218 --> 00:02:56,762
This jar holds oxygen molecules and carbon dioxide

78
00:02:56,803 --> 00:02:58,704
molecules, moving around together.

79
00:02:59,367 --> 00:03:02,205
It contains only carbon and oxygen atoms.

80
00:03:02,810 --> 00:03:05,018
So is it an element, a compound

81
00:03:05,562 --> 00:03:06,143
or a mixture?

82
00:03:06,773 --> 00:03:07,159
Have a think.

83
00:03:10,892 --> 00:03:11,522
It's a mixture.

84
00:03:12,109 --> 00:03:13,855
Look at the particles, not the list of

85
00:03:13,937 --> 00:03:14,177
atoms.

86
00:03:14,841 --> 00:03:16,860
There are two different kinds of particle here.

87
00:03:17,444 --> 00:03:20,357
Inside each carbon dioxide molecule, the atoms are

88
00:03:20,398 --> 00:03:21,824
bonded in a fixed ratio.

89
00:03:22,347 --> 00:03:25,133
But the carbon dioxide molecules aren't bonded to

90
00:03:25,175 --> 00:03:26,337
the oxygen molecules.

91
00:03:26,979 --> 00:03:29,484
And you could add more oxygen, or less,

92
00:03:29,967 --> 00:03:32,512
so the overall ratio of carbon to oxygen

93
00:03:32,773 --> 00:03:33,354
can change.

94
00:03:33,977 --> 00:03:35,624
In a compound, it never can.

95
00:03:36,442 --> 00:03:39,358
Mixtures split once more, by how evenly they're

96
00:03:39,378 --> 00:03:39,660
mixed.

97
00:03:40,243 --> 00:03:42,272
Stir salt into water until it disappears.

98
00:03:42,934 --> 00:03:45,084
The salt breaks up into its ions, and

99
00:03:45,104 --> 00:03:47,355
they spread evenly between the water molecules.

100
00:03:48,097 --> 00:03:50,384
Every drop has the same composition, and you

101
00:03:50,424 --> 00:03:51,768
can't see separate parts.

102
00:03:52,470 --> 00:03:55,139
That's a homogeneous mixture, the same all the

103
00:03:55,179 --> 00:03:55,561
way through.

104
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:57,137
Air is one too.

105
00:03:58,085 --> 00:03:59,831
Now look at the iron and sulfur again.

106
00:04:00,414 --> 00:04:03,104
There are grey patches and yellow patches, and

107
00:04:03,145 --> 00:04:05,112
a scoop from one side isn't the same

108
00:04:05,193 --> 00:04:06,176
as a scoop from the other.

109
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:08,348
That's a heterogeneous mixture.

110
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:11,099
Its composition differs from region to region.

111
00:04:11,668 --> 00:04:13,135
Here's the classic exam trap.

112
00:04:13,638 --> 00:04:16,588
Pure water and salt water look identical.

113
00:04:17,051 --> 00:04:18,664
Both are clear and uniform.

114
00:04:19,311 --> 00:04:21,799
But uniform only tells you a mixture is

115
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:22,421
homogeneous.

116
00:04:22,903 --> 00:04:24,830
It doesn't tell you a sample is pure.

117
00:04:25,635 --> 00:04:28,220
The salt water is still two substances, and

118
00:04:28,261 --> 00:04:30,205
you can make it saltier or less salty.

119
00:04:30,989 --> 00:04:32,114
Alloys are another trap.

120
00:04:32,919 --> 00:04:36,073
Gold-coloured Australian coins are mostly copper, with some

121
00:04:36,174 --> 00:04:37,178
aluminium and nickel.

122
00:04:37,781 --> 00:04:40,406
Those atoms are spread evenly, but the proportions

123
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:43,011
are chosen by the maker, not fixed by

124
00:04:43,052 --> 00:04:43,512
a formula.

125
00:04:44,154 --> 00:04:46,541
So an alloy is a homogeneous mixture,

126
00:04:47,043 --> 00:04:47,804
not a compound.

127
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:50,385
Here's a way to sort any sample.

128
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:52,882
Ask the same question three times:

129
00:04:53,527 --> 00:04:54,029
just one?

130
00:04:54,735 --> 00:04:56,227
Is there just one substance?

131
00:04:56,833 --> 00:04:59,288
If yes, it's pure, and if not, it's

132
00:04:59,309 --> 00:04:59,691
a mixture.

133
00:05:00,356 --> 00:05:01,378
For a pure substance,

134
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:03,205
is there just one element?

135
00:05:03,769 --> 00:05:04,894
Then it's an element.

136
00:05:05,478 --> 00:05:07,571
Two or more, bonded in a fixed ratio,

137
00:05:07,774 --> 00:05:08,739
make a compound.

138
00:05:09,403 --> 00:05:10,126
And for a mixture,

139
00:05:10,629 --> 00:05:13,099
is it just one region, the same all

140
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:13,662
the way through?

141
00:05:14,207 --> 00:05:15,338
Then it's homogeneous.

142
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:17,582
If not, it's heterogeneous.

143
00:05:20,290 --> 00:05:20,672
Your turn.

144
00:05:21,458 --> 00:05:23,719
Classify each sample as an element,

145
00:05:24,221 --> 00:05:24,800
a compound, a

146
00:05:25,361 --> 00:05:26,301
homogeneous mixture

147
00:05:26,823 --> 00:05:28,103
or a heterogeneous mixture,

148
00:05:28,624 --> 00:05:31,506
and justify each answer using its particles.

149
00:05:32,491 --> 00:05:34,725
Solid sulfur, made of S eight molecules.

150
00:05:35,450 --> 00:05:37,881
Glucose, C six H twelve O six.

151
00:05:38,686 --> 00:05:40,273
And sand stirred into water.

152
00:05:41,059 --> 00:05:41,824
Pause here to answer.

153
00:05:52,589 --> 00:05:53,715
Sulfur is an element.

154
00:05:54,238 --> 00:05:56,705
Each molecule has eight atoms, but they're all

155
00:05:56,806 --> 00:05:58,450
sulfur, one kind of atom.

156
00:05:59,153 --> 00:06:02,325
Glucose is a compound, three elements bonded in

157
00:06:02,346 --> 00:06:04,413
a fixed ratio in every molecule.

158
00:06:05,257 --> 00:06:08,163
Sand in water is a heterogeneous mixture, two

159
00:06:08,224 --> 00:06:10,649
substances, with the sand in its own region.

160
00:06:11,452 --> 00:06:13,917
The classic slip is calling sulfur a compound

161
00:06:14,179 --> 00:06:15,943
because its molecule has eight atoms.

162
00:06:16,545 --> 00:06:18,241
It's the kinds of atom that count.

163
00:06:20,077 --> 00:06:20,439
Last one.

164
00:06:21,205 --> 00:06:23,274
A student makes two sugar solutions.

165
00:06:23,897 --> 00:06:25,898
One has five grams of sugar in every

166
00:06:26,019 --> 00:06:28,240
hundred grams of solution, and the other has

167
00:06:28,321 --> 00:06:30,362
fifteen grams in every hundred grams.

168
00:06:31,026 --> 00:06:32,759
Both are clear and look the same all

169
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:33,283
the way through.

170
00:06:34,106 --> 00:06:36,607
The student says sugar solution is a pure

171
00:06:36,668 --> 00:06:38,249
substance, because it's uniform.

172
00:06:38,871 --> 00:06:39,898
Evaluate this claim.

173
00:06:40,462 --> 00:06:41,285
Pause here to answer.

174
00:06:53,676 --> 00:06:54,441
The claim is wrong.

175
00:06:55,547 --> 00:06:58,274
Uniform tells you it's a homogeneous mixture, not

176
00:06:58,315 --> 00:06:59,076
a pure substance.

177
00:06:59,679 --> 00:07:02,011
There are two kinds of particle, sugar molecules

178
00:07:02,254 --> 00:07:03,138
and water molecules.

179
00:07:03,923 --> 00:07:05,146
And the composition changes,

180
00:07:05,668 --> 00:07:07,673
five percent sugar in one and fifteen percent

181
00:07:07,714 --> 00:07:08,075
in the other.

182
00:07:08,617 --> 00:07:10,276
A pure substance can't do that.

183
00:07:10,902 --> 00:07:12,289
So it's a homogeneous mixture.

184
00:07:12,793 --> 00:07:15,129
Clear and uniform never proves pure.

185
00:07:16,949 --> 00:07:19,166
In the lab, though, you can't see particles.

186
00:07:19,671 --> 00:07:21,688
So we need evidence we can actually measure.

187
00:07:22,574 --> 00:07:25,041
Pure water and salt water look the same,

188
00:07:25,464 --> 00:07:27,308
but heat them and they don't behave the

189
00:07:27,350 --> 00:07:27,569
same.

190
00:07:28,253 --> 00:07:30,399
Next time, we'll find out what melting and

191
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:32,205
boiling can tell us about a sample.

192
00:07:34,961 --> 00:07:35,784
Here are the key notes.

193
00:07:36,307 --> 00:07:37,855
Pause the video if you need more time

194
00:07:37,895 --> 00:07:38,317
to copy them.
